"""Phase 5c copy, batch 1: check-in builder, onboarding builder, offer builder, GLP-1 planner. Original prose (ADR 0036)."""
from phase5_common import base, emit, s

D = {}

D["client-check-in-builder"] = base(
    "client-check-in-builder",
    eyebrow="Free tool · Client tracking",
    title="Client check-in builder for coaches",
    h1="Client check-in builder",
    description="Build a structured weekly check-in form for coaching clients from their goal, the metrics you track and the depth you want, ready to copy.",
    intro="Choose your client's goal, the things you track and how detailed you want it, and get a weekly check-in you can copy and send. Numbers come first, then a few short reflection questions, laid out so you can review each one in minutes. Your choices stay in your browser.",
    q="What should a weekly coaching check-in include?",
    a="A few numbers that match the client's goal and a few short questions. For fat loss, that's usually average weight, waist, days on plan, sessions done, sleep and steps, followed by what went well, what was hard and what they'd like help with. Keep it short enough to complete in five minutes and consistent enough to compare week to week.",
    sections=[
        s("Why structure matters",
          "Freeform check-ins, where clients write whatever comes to mind, are friendly but slow to read and hard to compare. One week a client mentions their weight, the next they don't. One week they describe every meal, the next they write a single line. A structured check-in asks for the same numbers in the same order each week, so you can scan it in a minute, spot changes and compare it with last week at a glance.",
          "Structure helps clients too. A clear form is quicker to fill in, and the same questions every week build a habit of reflecting on what worked."),
        s("Numbers first, then reflection",
          "The builder puts the numbers at the top: weight, adherence, training, sleep, steps and whatever else you track. These give you the facts. Then it adds a few short reflection questions, which give you the context behind the facts: why a week was hard, what they're proud of, what they want help with. Together they make a good coaching decision much easier.",
          "Keeping reflection questions short and specific gets better answers. 'How was your week?' often gets 'fine'. 'Where did the plan feel hardest, and what got in the way?' gets something you can act on."),
        s("Choosing what to track",
          ("table", ["Goal", "Default metrics"], [["Fat loss", "Weight and measurements, nutrition, training, sleep, steps"], ["Muscle gain", "Weight and measurements, nutrition, training, sleep"], ["Recomposition", "Weight and measurements, nutrition, training, progress photos"], ["Performance", "Training, sleep, energy and mood, nutrition"], ["Health and habits", "Habits, steps, sleep, energy and mood"], ["Wellness", "Energy and mood, sleep, habits"]]),
          "Change the defaults freely. Track only what you'll actually use: every extra question makes the check-in longer for the client and longer for you to review."),
        s("Choosing depth",
          ("ul", [
              "**Quick scan:** one line per metric and one question. Best for established clients doing well, or very busy ones.",
              "**Standard:** a second detail for each metric and three questions. Suits most clients most of the time.",
              "**Deep:** extra details and four questions. Useful at the start of coaching, at a review point or when something isn't working.",
          ]),
          "You can change depth over time. Many coaches start new clients on a standard or deep check-in for the first month, then move to quick scans once things are steady."),
        s("Weight and measurements",
          "For weight, ask for an average of morning weigh-ins across the week rather than a single reading. Averages smooth out the daily swings from water and food. For measurements, the waist at the navel is the most useful single number for fat loss; hips add context. Ask clients to measure the same way each time, ideally on the same day."),
        s("Nutrition and training",
          "Days on plan out of seven is a simple, honest measure of nutrition adherence that clients can answer quickly. Average protein adds detail where it matters. For training, sessions done versus planned shows consistency, and a best lift or session gives you, and them, something positive to notice. A question about pain or niggles catches problems early."),
        s("Sleep, steps, energy and mood",
          "Sleep and stress often explain why a week went well or badly. A quick rating of sleep quality, energy and stress, each on a 1 to 5 scale, takes seconds to answer and gives you an early warning when recovery is slipping. Steps show how active they are outside training, which matters a great deal for fat loss."),
        s("Progress photos and cycle tracking",
          "Photos show changes the scale and tape miss. Ask for the same angles, light, time and clothing each time, and only if the client is comfortable. For clients who menstruate, noting the cycle phase helps explain water retention, appetite and energy changes, and stops a normal pre-period weight rise from being read as a stall."),
        s("Using the check-in",
          "Send the check-in on the same day each week and review it within a set time, such as 24 or 48 hours. Reply with one clear piece of feedback and one change or focus for the coming week. The client check-in reader on this site can help turn the answers into a single decision.",
          "Consistency is what makes check-ins powerful. A simple check-in done every week, with a thoughtful reply, keeps clients far longer than an elaborate one done occasionally."),
    ],
    faq=[
        ("How long should a check-in be?", "Short enough to complete in about five minutes. Most clients do best with five to eight numbers and two to four questions."),
        ("How often should clients check in?", "Weekly works for most online coaching. Some coaches use fortnightly check-ins for established clients."),
        ("Should I ask for progress photos?", "If the client is comfortable. Use the same angles, light and clothing each time, and keep them private."),
        ("Can I change the questions?", "Yes. Copy the check-in and edit it to match your coaching style and each client's needs."),
        ("Is anything stored?", "No. The builder runs in your browser and nothing is saved."),
    ],
    pitch_h="Run this check-in every week",
    pitch="In Traiv, clients send their check-in in the app with answers, ratings, weight, measurements and photos, and you review it beside their plan and reply from the same place.",
    nxt=[("Client check-in reader", "/tools/client-check-in-reader"), ("Client onboarding builder", "/tools/client-onboarding-builder"), ("Weight trend reader", "/tools/weight-trend-reader")],
)

D["client-onboarding-builder"] = base(
    "client-onboarding-builder",
    eyebrow="Free tool · Client workflow",
    title="Client onboarding builder for coaches",
    h1="Client onboarding builder",
    description="Build a step-by-step onboarding sequence for new coaching clients, from welcome and intake to the first programme and check-in.",
    intro="Choose the type of coaching, the steps you run and how long onboarding should take, and get a day-by-day sequence you can copy into your checklist. It covers everything from the welcome message and intake to the first programme and first check-in. The first weeks decide whether a new client stays; this makes sure nothing is forgotten. Your choices stay in your browser.",
    q="What should a coaching onboarding process include?",
    a="A welcome message, payment and terms, an intake form and health questionnaire, baseline measurements, app setup, communication norms, reviewing the intake, goal-setting, the first programme, an onboarding call and the first check-in. Spread them across the first few days or two weeks so the client feels looked after without being overwhelmed.",
    sections=[
        s("Why onboarding matters",
          "A new client arrives motivated and a little uncertain. What happens in the next few days shapes how they feel about the whole coaching relationship. A clear, warm, organised start builds confidence; a slow or confusing one plants doubt. Many clients who leave in the first two months do so because onboarding never quite got them going.",
          "A written sequence makes onboarding consistent. Your tenth client gets the same careful start as your first, even in a busy week."),
        s("The eleven steps",
          ("table", ["Step", "Purpose"], [["Welcome message", "Set a warm tone and explain what happens next"], ["Payment and terms", "Confirm payment and agree how coaching works"], ["Intake and PAR-Q", "Understand goals, history and health"], ["Baseline and photos", "Record a starting point to compare against"], ["App setup", "Make sure they can find their plan and message you"], ["Communication norms", "Agree reply times, check-in day and urgent contact"], ["Review intake", "Read the intake properly and note follow-ups"], ["Goal-setting", "Agree an outcome goal and behaviour goals"], ["Deliver first programme", "Give them the first plan, with a lighter first week"], ["Onboarding call", "Walk through the plan and answer questions"], ["First check-in", "Review, reply personally and make one adjustment"]]),
          "Untick any step you don't use, and the sequence adjusts."),
        s("Choosing a timeline",
          ("ul", [
              "**First 3 days:** a fast start for clients who are ready to go and want momentum.",
              "**First week:** a balanced pace for most clients, with time to review the intake properly.",
              "**First two weeks:** a gentler pace with a second check-in on day 14, so the first fortnight ends on a visible win.",
          ]),
          "Whatever the timeline, keep the client informed: tell them what's next and when."),
        s("The welcome message",
          "The welcome message should arrive within hours of sign-up. Thank them, mention their goal, explain what happens in the next few days and when they'll hear from you, and list anything you need, such as the intake form. A personal touch, like their name and goal, makes it feel like coaching rather than an automated email."),
        s("Health screening",
          "A pre-exercise health questionnaire, such as the PAR-Q, catches conditions that need medical clearance before training. Ask about heart conditions, chest pain, dizziness, joint problems, medication, pregnancy and recent surgery. If anything is flagged, ask the client to get clearance from their doctor before starting. The scope-of-practice check on this site can help decide what to do."),
        s("Goal-setting that works",
          "Agree one outcome goal, such as losing 5 kg or squatting body weight, and two or three behaviour goals the client controls, such as three sessions a week and protein at every meal. Outcome goals give direction; behaviour goals give the client something to succeed at every week. Write them down and set a date to review them."),
        s("The first programme",
          "Make the first week lighter than you think it needs to be. New clients are learning exercises, finding their routine and getting used to the app. A first week that feels achievable builds confidence; one that leaves them exhausted or confused builds doubt. Include a short note explaining how to use the plan and what to focus on first."),
        s("The onboarding call",
          "A short call of 20 to 30 minutes makes a big difference. Walk through the plan, show them where to find everything, answer questions and confirm the first check-in date. Clients who have spoken to their coach feel a much stronger connection than those who only receive documents."),
        s("The first check-in",
          "The first check-in is a chance to show that the plan responds to them. Reply personally, mention something specific they did well and make one small adjustment based on what they told you. That single moment, when a client sees their feedback change the plan, often decides whether they trust the coaching."),
    ],
    faq=[
        ("How long should client onboarding take?", "Usually three days to two weeks, depending on the client and how much setup is needed."),
        ("What is a PAR-Q?", "A Physical Activity Readiness Questionnaire: a short health screening to check whether someone should get medical clearance before exercising."),
        ("Should I include an onboarding call?", "Yes, if you can. A short call builds connection and prevents confusion."),
        ("Can I edit the sequence?", "Yes. Copy it and adapt it to your coaching and tools."),
        ("Is anything saved?", "No. The builder runs in your browser."),
    ],
    pitch_key="client-app",
    pitch_h="Give new clients one place to start",
    pitch="In Traiv, new clients join with a code, find their plan, check-ins and chat in one app under your name, and you can see from your roster who has started.",
    keep=[("The client app in Traiv", "/features/client-app"), ("All free tools", "/tools")],
    nxt=[("Client check-in builder", "/tools/client-check-in-builder"), ("Coaching offer builder", "/tools/coaching-offer-builder"), ("Coaching scope-of-practice check", "/tools/scope-of-practice-check")],
)

D["coaching-offer-builder"] = base(
    "coaching-offer-builder",
    eyebrow="Free tool · Offer and sales",
    title="Coaching offer builder",
    h1="Coaching offer builder",
    description="Build a clear, specific coaching offer from your model, commitment length and what's included, ready for a sales page, a message or a call.",
    intro="Choose your coaching model, how long the commitment is and what's included, and get a clear offer someone can read in seconds and understand exactly what they'll get. Specific offers sell far better than vague ones. Paste it into a sales page, a message or your discovery call notes. Your choices stay in your browser.",
    q="How do I write a clear coaching offer?",
    a="State who it's for, what's included, how long it lasts, what you need from the client and the next step. List concrete things, such as a training programme built around their schedule, a weekly check-in with personal feedback and messaging support with an agreed reply time, rather than general promises. Clients buy what they can picture.",
    sections=[
        s("Why specific offers sell",
          "Many coaching offers say something like 'personalised coaching to help you reach your goals'. That's true of every coach, so it tells the reader nothing. A specific offer lists exactly what the client gets, how often and for how long. The reader can picture their week with you, which makes deciding much easier.",
          "Specific offers also prevent misunderstandings later. When what's included is written down, there's less room for disappointment or requests for extras."),
        s("The parts of an offer",
          ("ol", [
              "**Headline:** the model and length, so the reader knows what kind of offer this is.",
              "**Who it's for:** one sentence describing the person it suits.",
              "**What's included:** a short list of concrete deliverables.",
              "**Commitment:** how long, and what happens at the end.",
              "**What they bring:** what you expect from the client.",
              "**Next step:** one clear action, such as booking a short call.",
          ]),
          "The builder lays these out in order. Add your price and any personal details before using it."),
        s("Choosing a model",
          ("table", ["Model", "Best for"], [["1:1 coaching", "Clients who want a plan built around them and personal feedback"], ["Group coaching", "Clients who want structure and community at a lower price"], ["Hybrid", "Clients who want personal coaching plus group support"], ["Self-led programme", "Independent clients who want a clear plan without regular contact"]]),
          "Many coaches offer two models: a premium one-to-one option and a lower-priced group or self-led option."),
        s("Choosing a length",
          "Monthly offers are easy to start but easy to leave. Fixed commitments of 12 weeks, 16 weeks or 6 months give clients time to see real change and give you more stable income. Longer commitments often come with a lower monthly equivalent; the coaching package pricing calculator on this site can help set prices for each length."),
        s("What to include",
          "Include only what you'll reliably deliver. It's better to promise a weekly check-in and deliver it every week than to promise daily support and fall behind. Common inclusions are a training programme, a nutrition plan, weekly check-ins, messaging support, a client app, progress tracking, form checks, an onboarding call, habit coaching and community access.",
          "Each extra inclusion adds time. Price the offer for the time it takes, or keep the list short."),
        s("Writing for the reader",
          "Write in plain, concrete language from the client's side: 'a training programme written for your schedule' rather than 'bespoke programming solutions'. Avoid jargon and superlatives. Describe what happens each week, so the reader can imagine it."),
        s("Using the offer",
          "Put the offer on your sales page, send it after a discovery call, pin it in your social profile or use it as a script for explaining your coaching. Keep the wording consistent everywhere, so prospects hear the same thing wherever they find you."),
        s("Testing and improving",
          "Watch how prospects respond. If they often ask the same question, answer it in the offer. If they hesitate over the length, try offering a shorter starter option. If they ask about something you don't include, decide whether to add it or explain why not. Offers improve with each conversation."),
    ],
    faq=[
        ("What should a coaching offer include?", "Who it's for, what's included, how long it lasts, what the client brings and the next step."),
        ("Should I put the price in the offer?", "Many coaches do, to filter out poor fits. Others share it on the call. Either way, be clear and consistent."),
        ("How many offers should I have?", "Two or three at most: for example, a premium one-to-one option and a lower-priced group or self-led option."),
        ("Is anything stored?", "No. The builder runs in your browser."),
    ],
    pitch_key="client-app",
    pitch_h="Deliver everything you promised in one place",
    pitch="In Traiv, the programme, nutrition plan, check-ins and chat you list in your offer are delivered in one app under your name, so clients get exactly what they bought.",
    keep=[("The client app in Traiv", "/features/client-app"), ("All free tools", "/tools")],
    nxt=[("Coaching package pricing calculator", "/tools/coaching-package-pricing-calculator"), ("Sales objection handler", "/tools/sales-objection-handler"), ("Discovery call calculator", "/tools/discovery-call-calculator")],
)

D["glp1-client-coaching-planner"] = base(
    "glp1-client-coaching-planner",
    eyebrow="Free tool · Client issue",
    title="GLP-1 client coaching planner",
    h1="Coaching a client on a GLP-1 medication",
    description="Build a coaching plan for a client on a GLP-1 weight-loss medication: muscle-protecting training, protein strategies, habits and what stays with the prescriber.",
    intro="Clients on GLP-1 medications often lose weight quickly, and without the right support a large share of it can be muscle. The coach's role is the part the medication doesn't cover: protecting muscle through training, reaching protein when appetite is low, and building habits that last after the medication ends. Set where the client is and get a plan, with a clear line on what belongs with their prescriber. Not medical advice. Your choices stay in your browser.",
    q="How should I coach a client on a GLP-1 medication?",
    a="Focus on strength training two to four times a week, protein at every meal (often around 1.2 to 1.6 g per kg of body weight a day, adjusted with their prescriber if they have other conditions), fluids, daily steps and habits that will last. Watch strength, waist and protein at check-ins. Leave the medication, dose and side effects to the prescriber.",
    sections=[
        s("Why coaching matters on a GLP-1",
          "GLP-1 receptor agonists and related medications reduce appetite and can lead to substantial weight loss. Studies show that a meaningful share of the weight lost can be lean mass, including muscle. Losing muscle lowers strength, affects metabolism and can make weight regain more likely when the medication stops.",
          "Coaching addresses exactly that. Strength training signals the body to hold on to muscle, adequate protein supplies what muscle needs, and habits built during treatment help maintain results afterwards."),
        s("Four stages",
          ("table", ["Stage", "Coaching focus"], [["Just starting", "Build protein, strength and fluid habits early; expect side effects"], ["Mid-titration", "Keep training steady; protect protein as appetite falls further"], ["Stable dose", "Progressive strength training; protein most days"], ["Coming off or tapering", "Structured meals and training as appetite returns"]]),
          "The planner adjusts its focus to the stage you select."),
        s("Training to protect muscle",
          "Strength training is the single most important coaching tool for these clients. Two to four sessions a week, built around compound movements such as squats or leg presses, hinges, pushes, pulls and carries, gives muscle a reason to stay. The aim is to keep the weights steady or slowly rising while body weight falls.",
          "Adjust volume to energy. On low-appetite days, shorter sessions that stop a rep or two before failure are better than skipped sessions. Daily walking adds activity without much fatigue."),
        s("Protein when appetite is low",
          "Reaching protein targets is often the hardest part. Appetite can be very low, and large meals may cause discomfort. Practical strategies include leading each meal with protein, eating smaller, more frequent portions, choosing soft or liquid protein sources such as yoghurt, milk, eggs or shakes, and tracking protein alone rather than every calorie.",
          "A common target for adults losing weight is around 1.2 to 1.6 g per kg of body weight a day, but clients with kidney disease or other conditions need guidance from their prescriber or a dietitian."),
        s("Side effects and when to refer",
          "Nausea, constipation, reflux and fatigue are common, especially early on and after dose increases. Coaches can help with practical eating habits, such as eating slowly and avoiding very rich meals, but managing side effects medically belongs with the prescriber.",
          ("ul", [
              "Severe or persistent vomiting or diarrhoea",
              "Signs of dehydration, such as dizziness or very dark urine",
              "Severe abdominal pain, which can signal gallbladder or pancreas problems",
              "A client who can't eat enough to function or train",
          ]),
          "Refer any of these to the prescriber promptly."),
        s("What to watch at check-ins",
          "Track weight trend, waist and strength on the main lifts. Strength holding steady while weight falls is a good sign that muscle is being protected. Also track protein days, training sessions, energy and side effects. A sudden drop in strength, very rapid weight loss or persistent fatigue are reasons to review the plan and, where needed, talk to the prescriber."),
        s("Preparing for life after the medication",
          "Many people stop GLP-1 medications at some point, and appetite usually returns. Habits built during treatment, such as regular protein-rich meals, consistent strength training, daily movement and an awareness of hunger and fullness, make the difference between maintaining results and regaining weight. Start building these from the beginning, not at the end."),
        s("Staying in your lane",
          "The medication, dose, side-effect management and decisions about continuing or stopping belong with the prescriber. Coaches support training, food habits and behaviour. Coordinate where possible, encourage clients to report side effects, and keep a note of what's coaching and what's medical."),
    ],
    faq=[
        ("How much protein should a client on a GLP-1 eat?", "Often around 1.2 to 1.6 g per kg of body weight a day for adults losing weight, adjusted with their prescriber or dietitian if they have other conditions."),
        ("Should clients on GLP-1 medications lift weights?", "Yes, where safe. Strength training is the main way to protect muscle during rapid weight loss."),
        ("Can I advise on the medication or dose?", "No. That belongs with the prescriber. Coach training, food habits and behaviour."),
        ("When should I refer a client to their prescriber?", "For severe or persistent vomiting or diarrhoea, signs of dehydration, severe abdominal pain, or if they can't eat enough to function."),
    ],
    pitch_key="workouts",
    pitch_h="Deliver the muscle-protecting plan",
    pitch="In Traiv, the strength programme, nutrition targets and weekly check-ins for this client live in one app, so you can see whether strength is holding as their weight falls.",
    keep=[("Workout plans in Traiv", "/features/plan-builder"), ("All free tools", "/tools")],
    disclaimer="General coaching information only, not medical advice. The medication, dose, side effects and decisions to continue or stop are for the prescriber. Refer anything concerning promptly.",
    nxt=[("Coaching scope-of-practice check", "/tools/scope-of-practice-check"), ("Protein calculator", "/tools/protein-calculator"), ("Lean body mass calculator", "/tools/lean-body-mass-calculator")],
)

